    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/columba"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/columba.jpg" alt="Columba" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Columba_at_Bridei%27s_fort.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">J. R. Skelton (Joseph Ratcliffe Skelton; 1865–1927) (illustrator), erroneously credited as John R. Skelton, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> grew out of the union of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, who held most of the land north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-forth">Forth</a>, and the Gaelic kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dal-riata">Dál Riata</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argyll">Argyll</a>. Tradition dates the new realm to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Kenneth MacAlpin</a> (Cináed mac Ailpín), a king of Gaelic descent who became ruler of the Picts around 843 and died in 858. Medieval accounts later described a violent conquest of the Picts, but modern historians see a more gradual process in which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vikings">Viking</a> attacks on both peoples, intermarriage among royal kindreds and the spread of Gaelic language and church culture fused them into one realm. By about 900, under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donald-ii-of-scotland">Donald II</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantine-ii-of-scotland">Constantine II</a>, the kingdom was called Alba, with its royal centre at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scone-scotland">Scone</a> and relics of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/columba">Columba</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dunkeld">Dunkeld</a>. Over the following centuries its kings expanded southwards. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malcolm-ii-of-scotland">Malcolm II</a>’s victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-carham">Battle of Carham</a>, around 1018, secured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lothian">Lothian</a> between the Forth and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/river-tweed">Tweed</a>, and the British kingdom of Strathclyde was absorbed during the eleventh century. Galloway, Moray and the Norse-ruled north and west were brought under royal control only slowly: after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-largs">Battle of Largs</a> in 1263, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-perth">Treaty of Perth</a> of 1266 transferred the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hebrides">Western Isles</a> and the Isle of Man from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/norway">Norway</a> to Scotland, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orkney">Orkney</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shetland">Shetland</a> followed in 1468–1469.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed.jpg" alt="Berwick-upon-Tweed" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick-upon-Tweed</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Berwick-upon-Tweed_-_geograph.org.uk_-_6643184.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Walter Baxter, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was a thinly populated country, with pastoral farming dominant in the Highlands and mixed farming in the Lowlands, and it probably had around a million people by 1700. Its society combined Gaelic kin-based lordship, strongest in the Highlands and Islands, with feudal tenures introduced from the twelfth century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I and his successors invited Anglo-Norman and Flemish settlers. Royal burghs such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth-scotland">Perth</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> held monopolies of foreign trade, exporting wool, hides, fish and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coal">coal</a> and salt to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/low-countries">Low Countries</a>, the Baltic and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>; from the fifteenth century a Scottish staple port in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-the-netherlands">Netherlands</a>, from 1541 at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veere">Veere</a>, handled much of this trade. Language divided the country: Gaelic remained dominant in the north and west, while Scots, which grew from northern English, became the language of the Lowlands, the court and the law. The church was rich, with great reformed abbeys such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melrose-abbey">Melrose</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kelso-abbey">Kelso</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holyrood-abbey">Holyrood</a>, and the founding of universities at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-andrews">St Andrews</a> (1413), <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> (1451) and Aberdeen (1495) gave Scotland three universities when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> still had two. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of 1560 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">the Kirk</a> aimed at a school in every parish, a goal written into law by an act of 1696. Many Scots sought a living abroad as soldiers, merchants and pedlars in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/poland">Poland</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and Ireland.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stirling-bridge"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-stirling-bridge.jpg" alt="Battle of Stirling Bridge" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stirling-bridge">Battle of Stirling Bridge</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:The_Battle_of_Stirling_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Matthew White Ridley, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>’s armies were raised from the common army of free men owing service, the feudal knights of the Lowlands and the clan levies of the Highlands, and they were rarely a match for English armies in pitched battle. Their strength lay in defensive warfare, the spear formations known as schiltrons, and raiding into northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>. The Wars of Independence began when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-i-of-england">Edward I of England</a> invaded in 1296, sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berwick-upon-tweed">Berwick</a> and took the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stone-of-scone">Stone of Scone</a> to Westminster. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-wallace">William Wallace</a> defeated an English army at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-stirling-bridge">Battle of Stirling Bridge</a> in 1297 but lost at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-falkirk-1298">Falkirk</a> in 1298. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-the-bruce">Robert the Bruce</a>, crowned in 1306, waged a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> war and took castles one by one before routing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-ii-of-england">Edward II</a> at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-bannockburn">Battle of Bannockburn</a> in 1314. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/second-war-of-scottish-independence">Second War of Independence</a> brought defeats at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-halidon-hill">Halidon Hill</a> in 1333 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-nevilles-cross">Neville’s Cross</a> in 1346, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david-ii-of-scotland">David II</a> was captured. The alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auld-alliance">Auld Alliance</a> first sealed in 1295, drew Scotland into repeated wars with England: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-iv-of-scotland">James IV</a> built a royal navy, including the great ship <em>Michael</em>, but was killed with much of his nobility at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-flodden">Battle of Flodden</a> in 1513, and another heavy defeat followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-solway-moss">Solway Moss</a> in 1542. In the 1640s the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/covenanters">Covenanting</a> army was among the strongest in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Oliver Cromwell</a> crushed Scottish forces at Dunbar in 1650 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/worcester">Worcester</a> in 1651.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/david"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/david.jpg" alt="David" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:King_David,_the_King_of_Israel.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gerard van Honthorst, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Scottish kings ruled through a small, personal government rather than a large bureaucracy. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a> I (1124–1153) laid the foundations of the medieval state, developing sheriffdoms, founding royal burghs, issuing the first Scottish coinage and organising the church into territorial dioceses and parishes. The crown passed within the royal house, but repeated royal minorities in the fifteenth and sixteenth centuries left regents and noble factions to govern, and several kings met violent ends, among them James I, murdered in 1437. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a>, a single chamber of the estates of clergy, nobility and burgh commissioners, developed from the thirteenth century; much of its business was prepared by a committee, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lords-of-the-articles">Lords of the Articles</a>. Scots law drew on Roman and canon law as well as native custom, and in 1532 James V founded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/college-of-justice">College of Justice</a>, whose Court of Session remains <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>’s supreme civil court. In the localities great <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnates</a> held hereditary jurisdictions, and in the Highlands clan chiefs ruled with considerable independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> of 1560 created a national <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-scotland">Kirk</a> governed, after a long conflict over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a>, by Presbyterian courts of kirk sessions, presbyteries, synods and a General Assembly. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/union-of-the-crowns">Union of the Crowns</a> in 1603, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI</a> governed Scotland from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a> through the Privy Council in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>, boasting that he ruled it with his pen.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath.jpg" alt="Declaration of Arbroath" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath">Declaration of Arbroath</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Declaration_of_arbroath.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Scotland barons, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>’s long resistance to English conquest made it a rare case of a small medieval kingdom preserving its independence beside a much larger neighbour. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/declaration-of-arbroath">Declaration of Arbroath</a> of 1320, a letter from the barons to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-john-xxii">Pope John XXII</a> asserting the kingdom’s freedom and the right to replace a king who betrayed it, became one of the most quoted documents of Scottish history. The kingdom produced a distinctive literature in Scots, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-barbour">John Barbour</a>’s verse epic <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-brus">The Bruce</a></em> of the 1370s to the makars of the late fifteenth and early sixteenth centuries, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/robert-henryson">Robert Henryson</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-dunbar">William Dunbar</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gavin-douglas">Gavin Douglas</a>, whose translation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/virgil">Virgil</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aeneid">Aeneid</a></em> was completed in 1513. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protestant-reformation">Reformation</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-knox">John Knox</a> produced a Calvinist church that shaped Scottish society, education and ideas of resistance, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-covenant">National Covenant</a> of 1638 helped to start the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-three-kingdoms">Wars of the Three Kingdoms</a> that convulsed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> and Ireland. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI</a> became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> in 1603, a Scottish dynasty came to rule all of Britain and Ireland, and Scots settled in large numbers in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plantation-of-ulster">Plantation of Ulster</a> from 1609. The institutions the kingdom kept after 1707, its law, church and universities, became the base for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scottish-enlightenment">Scottish Enlightenment</a> of the eighteenth century and for a lasting Scottish national identity within the United Kingdom.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i.jpg" alt="James VI and I" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI and I</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:JamesIEngland_(cropped_2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Attributed to John de Critz, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The road to union began in 1603, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI</a> inherited the English throne as James I and moved his court to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/london">London</a>. The two kingdoms shared a monarch but kept separate parliaments, churches and laws, and the arrangement proved unstable. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-i-of-england">Charles I</a>’s attempt to impose a new prayer book in 1637 provoked the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-covenant">National Covenant</a> of 1638 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">Bishops</a>’ Wars, and in the civil wars that followed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> was conquered by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oliver-cromwell">Cromwell</a> and incorporated into the Commonwealth from 1652 until the Restoration of 1660. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glorious-revolution">Revolution of 1688</a>–1689 the Scottish Parliament became more assertive, while economic weakness grew acute: the famines of the 1690s, known as the ill years, and the failure of the Darien scheme, a colony on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/isthmus-of-panama">Isthmus of Panama</a> in 1698–1700 that lost a large share of the country’s capital, convinced many that access to English markets was essential. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, at war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, wanted to secure the Protestant succession and prevent Scotland from choosing a different monarch. After negotiations in 1706, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/parliament-of-scotland">Parliament of Scotland</a> ratified the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acts-of-union-1707">Treaty of Union</a>, and on 1 May 1707 Scotland and England were united as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a>, with one parliament at Westminster. The kingdom ended, but its law, Presbyterian church and education system were guaranteed, and a devolved Scottish Parliament was established in 1999.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> preserved its independence against a far larger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> for four centuries, created a distinctive law, church, literature and university tradition, and through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/james-vi-and-i">James VI</a> gave <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> its first shared monarch before the Union of 1707 merged it into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Kingdom of Great Britain</a>.</em></p>

